{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' docs/design/dao.jing.v2.implementation-plan.md | nl -ba -v1\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# dao.jing on DaoStream v2 — the content-observer slice\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: migration plan, revision 2, derived from and subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) (the contract), [`dao.jing.md`](./dao.jing.md)\n     5\t(the storage-observer design) and [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). Its\n     6\ttransport prerequisite is\n     7\t[`dao.stream.implementation-plan.md`](./dao.stream.implementation-plan.md),\n     8\tPhases 1–5, implemented on clj, cljs (Node) and cljd. It follows the shape of\n     9\tthe `dao.runtime` and `yin.repl` plans and is the fifth consumer\n    10\tmigration. This document is transient: it is consumed as its phases complete.\n    11\tDrafted 2026-09-06; revised the same day against the `gpt-5.6-sol` routine\n    12\treview, the `glm-5.3` adversarial review, and a two-round consensus with\n    13\t`gpt-6-astra`. No phase has started.\n    14\t\n    15\tTwo decisions in this document are **scope-contingent**: they are the plan's\n    16\tposition and are taken when implementation is authorized, each with its named\n    17\talternative. Neither blocks any phase. They are marked where they occur (J1,\n    18\tunder C2 and N1) and collected in *Boundary of this plan*.\n    19\t\n    20\t## Context\n    21\t\n    22\t`dao.jing` is next because `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.schema` require it,\n    23\tand `dao.space` on the v2 contract is what ends ADR-0003's time-boxed\n    24\texception. The v1 surface inside `dao.jing*` is small — one `ds/next` in the\n    25\tobserver walk (`jing.cljc:391`) and five sites in the file backend\n    26\t(`jing/file.cljc:83,149,175,190,207`) — with no `closed?`, `strict-vec`,\n    27\t`drain-one!` or `take!!` anywhere. A mechanical port is not what makes it hard.\n    28\tTwo of the three stream-touching pieces have no v2 counterpart to port *to*:\n    29\tthere is no v2 append-log transport, and there is no v2 RPC that returns a\n    30\tvalue synchronously. Each is a boundary decision, settled below before any\n    31\tphase runs.\n    32\t\n    33\tOne fact that shaped the revision: the v1 observer (`dao.jing/observer-state`,\n    34\t`observe-step!`) has **no consumer under `src/`**. Every caller is a test. The\n    35\t`dao.stream` require at `jing.cljc:19` exists solely for `ds/next` at\n    36\t`jing.cljc:391`, which only tests reach.\n    37\t\n    38\t## The problem, by piece\n    39\t\n    40\t| piece                                                    | v1 mechanism                                                                                               | v2 counterpart                                                            |\n    41\t| -------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n    42\t| `dao.jing/observer-state`, `observe-step!`               | `ds/next` on any v1 reader; inline `{:position 0}` cursors; bare `:ok`/`:blocked`/`:end`/`:daostream/gap`  | reader surface: `cursor`, `next`; opaque cursors; outcome maps            |\n    43\t| `dao.jing.file/create-content-file`                      | `ds/open!` of `{:dao.stream/type :append-log}`; `ds/next` to replay; `ds/append!` + fsync; `ds/close!`     | **none** — the stream plan defers file/log transports                     |\n    44\t| `dao.jing.remote/connect-content!`, `content-client`     | `dao.stream.rpc.client/call!` — synchronous on the JVM, Promise/Future elsewhere; JVM-only constructor     | `dao.stream.rpc` — poll-shaped, state-machine-valued, all three hosts  |\n    45\t| `dao.jing.remote/default-handlers`                       | handler map `{op (fn [& args] value)}`                                                                     | same shape; `dao.stream.apply/dispatch-request` applies it unchanged   |\n    46\t| `dao.jing.mem`, `dao.jing.coordinate`, hashing, `materialize!`, `get`, `close!` | stream-free                                                                         | unchanged                                                                 |\n    47\t\n    48\tConsumers outside `dao.jing*` that reach these pieces, named so the boundary is\n    49\tvisible. Nine `src/` namespaces require `dao.jing` — `dao.data.btree.storage`,\n    50\t`dao.space.index`, `dao.space.schema`, `dao.jing.{file,mem,remote,dht,dht.node}`\n    51\tand `src/dev/psset_fixtures.clj` — **none for the observer**. `dao.space.index`\n    52\tadditionally reaches `jing-coordinate/open!` inside its `defopen` and appends\n    53\tto the intake pool with v1 `ds/append!`. Seven test files call the v1 observer:\n    54\tfive under `test/dao/space/` (`transactor`, `stigmergy`, `index`, `schema`,\n    55\t`query`) plus `test/dao/jing/mem_test.cljc:213` and\n    56\t`test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:402`. `test/dao/space/` also opens remote clients\n    57\tthrough `connect-content!`. No production consumer is migrated here.\n    58\t\n    59\t## Strategy\n    60\t\n    61\tThe same two narrowings as the sibling plans, with one deliberate difference in\n    62\twhere the v2 namespace boundary falls.\n    63\t\n    64\t1. **Parallel where a v1 consumer still needs the v1 shape; in place where\n    65\t   nothing is being replaced.** The four prior consumers were rebuilt wholesale\n    66\t   beside their v1 twins because their entire surface was stream-shaped. Most\n    67\t   of `dao.jing` is not: content addressing, `materialize!`, `get`, `close!`\n    68\t   and `dao.jing.mem` never touch a stream. Duplicating them would create a\n    69\t   second minting path for content addresses — two sources of truth for\n    70\t   identity, which is worse than an unusual namespace layout. So:\n    71\t   - `dao.jing` becomes the **stream-free core** in J1, by moving the v1\n    72\t     observer out rather than by moving the core out (Decision 4, C2).\n    73\t   - The v1 observer moves, unchanged, to **`dao.jing.observer`** (v1), and\n    74\t     stays until `dao.space`'s tests migrate.\n    75\t   - The v2 observer is **`dao.jing.v2.observer`**, requiring `dao.jing` for\n    76\t     `materialize!` and `dao.stream` for the reader surface.\n    77\t   - The remote adapter is **`dao.jing.v2.remote`**, beside the untouched v1\n    78\t     `dao.jing.remote`, which `dao.jing.coordinate` and the `dao.space` tests\n    79\t     keep using until their own plan.\n    80\t   - `dao.jing.file` is rewritten **in place**: its migration is a removal of a\n    81\t     coupling, not a port (Decision 2). Its consumers use only\n    82\t     `create-content-file` and the handle API, which do not change.\n    83\t2. **The content-observer slice, and nothing else.** One observer, one durable\n    84\t   backend, one remote client with its handlers and a stepped materializer.\n    85\t   Not the canonical encoding, not durable checkpoints, not a content-serving\n    86\t   endpoint as production infrastructure, not `dao.space`'s intake appends,\n    87\t   not the DHT, not the content write path as an effect stream.\n    88\t\n    89\tNo compatibility facade, alias, or dual protocol. The v1 observer and v1\n    90\tremote are evidence about behavior, not constraints on the v2 shape.\n    91\t\n    92\t## Decisions\n    93\t\n    94\tSettled here so no phase decides them alone.\n    95\t\n    96\t### Decision 1 — The observer: entry shape, cursor discipline, gap\n    97\t\n    98\t**Entry shape.** A pool member is `{:stream <v2 reader handle>, :cursor <opaque>,\n    99\t:status s}`. The composition mints the cursor and hands both in:\n   100\t\n   101\t```clojure\n   102\t(observer/observer-state [{:stream h1 :cursor c1} {:stream h2 :cursor c2}])\n   103\t;; => {:members [{:stream h1 :cursor c1 :status :pending} ...] :next 0}\n   104\t```\n   105\t\n   106\t`observer-state` no longer takes bare streams and fabricates `{:position 0}`.\n   107\tIt cannot: every valid cursor comes from the stream, and the anchor is the\n   108\tcomposition's policy — `:dao.stream/oldest` for a fresh pool, a kept cursor for\n   109\ta resumed one — not the observer's. A member without a cursor, or whose stream\n   110\tdoes not satisfy the reader surface, is a composition defect detected before\n   111\tany operation runs and throws, which is the one place the contract permits an\n   112\texception. `observer-state` accepts exactly the member shape it stores, so\n   113\t`(observer-state (:members st))` rebuilds a pool from a previous state; that is\n   114\thow a composition drops a member without touching another member's cursor.\n   115\t\n   116\t**Cursor discipline.** The observer retains exactly the successor `next`\n   117\treturns, after `materialize!` succeeds, and never anything else. There is no\n   118\tarithmetic on a cursor anywhere in `dao.jing.v2.observer`, and no test inspects\n   119\ta cursor's shape — the conformance harness's own rule. The only other way a\n   120\tmember's cursor changes is `adopt-cursor`:\n   121\t\n   122\t```clojure\n   123\t(observer/adopt-cursor state i cursor) ; cursor came from the stream: minted, successor, or gap recovery\n   124\t```\n   125\t\n   126\twhich is the composition passing back a value the stream handed out.\n   127\t\n   128\t**Signals are the contract's outcome set, total.** `observe-step!` returns\n   129\t`{:state s' :signal k ...}` where `k` is one of the seven keywords in\n   130\t`dao.stream/outcomes-next`, and no other:\n   131\t\n   132\t| signal                                                   | when                                                                   | extra keys                          | member cursor |\n   133\t| -------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- | ------------- |\n   134\t| `:dao.stream/ok`                                         | a payload was materialized                                             | `:address`                          | successor     |\n   135\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`                                    | the pool is empty, or every non-ended member is blocked                | —                                   | unchanged     |\n   136\t| `:dao.stream/end`                                        | every member has ended                                                 | —                                   | unchanged     |\n   137\t| `:dao.stream/gap`                                        | member `i`'s position was evicted                                      | `:member i`, `:cursor` (recovery)   | unchanged     |\n   138\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch`, `/invalid-cursor`, `/transport-error` | member `i`'s read failed                                   | `:member i`, `:result` (the outcome map) | unchanged |\n   139\t\n   140\tThe last four are reported immediately, the member's `:status` records the\n   141\toutcome, the scheduling index moves past that member, and the member is polled\n   142\tagain on its next turn — and reports again. That is v1's gap rule (\"never\n   143\tauto-resynchronized\") applied to every non-`ok`/`blocked`/`end` outcome, one\n   144\trule instead of four. A `gap` on a content intake means payloads were appended,\n   145\tacknowledged to their publisher, and never materialized. Materialization is\n   146\tidempotent and replay-safe, so adopting the recovery cursor is *safe*; whether\n   147\tit is *acceptable* only the composition can answer, since it knows whether the\n   148\tpublisher will re-append. The observer must not answer it. `cursor-mismatch`\n   149\tand `invalid-cursor` are composition defects; `transport-error` may be\n   150\ttransient. All three are resolved the same way: the composition adopts a cursor\n   151\tit obtained from the stream, or rebuilds the pool without that member. A test\n   152\titerates `dao.stream/outcomes-next` and asserts every outcome produces a\n   153\tbranch, so a future contract outcome fails the test rather than falling into a\n   154\tdefault.\n   155\t\n   156\t**Materialization failure is unchanged.** `materialize!` throwing propagates\n   157\tand leaves the caller-owned state untouched. The storage backend is not a\n   158\tstream and is not under the stream contract; its failures stay exceptions, as\n   159\t`dao.jing.md` §Materialization rule already specifies.\n   160\t\n   161\t**What changes in `dao.jing.md`, and what stays.** Changes: *The intake pool*\n   162\t— the entry-shape sentence gains \"the cursor is minted by the composition from\n   163\tan anchor of its choosing, and is retained exactly as the stream returns it\";\n   164\t*Cursor tracking and recovery* — the four bare results become the seven\n   165\t`:dao.stream/…` outcomes with the table above, and the resync-is-the-caller's\n   166\tsentence is generalized to the three defect outcomes; *Implemented surface* —\n   167\tnames `dao.jing.observer` and the stream-free file backend (see Decision 2 for\n   168\tthe design-document change that entails); *Open items* — durable checkpoints\n   169\tgain the sentence that a checkpoint stores whatever cursor value the transport\n   170\tminted, and that whether such a value survives serialization is transport-owned\n   171\tand TBD in the contract. Stays, untouched: *Definition*, *Publication from an\n   172\tagent*, *Materialization rule*, *Canonical encoding*, *Storage ignorance*,\n   173\t*Physical intake versus semantic composition*, *Reads* (one clause added, see\n   174\tJ4), *Resource lifecycle*, *Lineage*. Nothing semantic about DaoJing moves; the\n   175\tmigration changes how the observer reads, not what it is.\n   176\t\n   177\t### Decision 2 — `dao.jing.file`: the durable log was never a stream (option b)\n   178\t\n   179\t**Decision.** The file backend's durable log is a storage implementation\n   180\tdetail. The migration removes the `dao.stream` coupling; it does not build a\n   181\tv2 append-log transport, and it does not use one.\n   182\t\n   183\t**This amends a design document.** `dao.jing.md:297-299` currently describes\n   184\tthe file backend as \"a content-addressed store backed by an append-only log\n   185\tstream.\" Decision 2 changes that description, and J4 rewrites it. That is a\n   186\tdesign-document change routed and reviewed as such, not an incidental edit\n   187\tmade in passing while updating the implemented-surface list. The superior\n   188\tdocuments `datom.world.md` and `dao.stream.md` are not amended.\n   189\t\n   190\t**Why, against §Storage ignorance and the passive well.** Four reasons, in\n   191\torder of weight:\n   192\t\n   193\t1. **The log has no second reader and no second writer, ever.** Inside\n   194\t   `create-content-file` the stream is opened, replayed once by its own\n   195\t   handle, appended by its own `put`, and closed by its own `close`. No\n   196\t   cursor is handed out, no descriptor is projected, nothing attaches, and\n   197\t   the log never sits in any pool. Every property the contract exists to\n   198\t   guarantee — independent cursors, non-destructive reads, portable\n   199\t   reachability, attachment lifecycle — is unused.\n   200\t2. **Making the on-disk layout a `dao.stream` transport made DaoJing's storage\n   201\t   format a public transport type.** `{:dao.stream/type :append-log}` is a\n   202\t   name any composition may open, so the record framing of a content file\n   203\t   became something other code could depend on. Removing the transport makes\n   204\t   the framing private to `dao.jing.file`.\n   205\t3. **A transport with one consumer owes the conformance suite for nobody.**\n   206\t   Option (a) would have this plan own a v2 append-log, its manifest, its\n   207\t   exclusion reasons, its inducers, and its reader laws on three hosts — for a\n   208\t   transport whose sole consumer never mints a second cursor.\n   209\t4. **The parallel is `dao.jing.mem`.** The in-memory backend holds its content\n   210\t   in a private atom behind `:put-content-fn`/`:get-content-fn`; the file\n   211\t   backend is the same handle with durability added. `dao.jing.md:278-282`\n   212\t   already says \"backend effects are explicit functions, not a protocol or\n   213\t   hidden state.\"\n   214\t\n   215\t**Why option (a) is not a like-for-like alternative.** The contract's IO model\n   216\tsays no operation waits (`dao.stream.md:153-158`), while `dao.jing.file`\n   217\tacknowledges a put only after the log is flushed and `materialize!` returns the\n   218\taddress only after the backend answers `:inserted`. A conforming v2 append-log\n   219\ttherefore could not wrap synchronous file writes and report durability from\n   220\t`append!`; durability would arrive later, as data. To sit beneath the file\n   221\tbackend it would force the content put to become an effect whose completion is\n   222\tobserved later — which means redesigning `materialize!`'s acknowledgement and\n   223\tevery consumer that calls it as a value-returning operation. Option (a) is the\n   224\twrite-path redesign in disguise, mis-costed as conformance-suite work.\n   225\t\n   226\t**The pre-existing question, recorded and routed.** `datom.world.md:66-68`\n   227\tsays: \"An adapter that exposes a function for portable code to call is not an\n   228\tinterpreter. It isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the\n   229\teffect never appears as an emission.\" `:put-content-fn` is such a function,\n   230\ttoday, on v1, with the log stream beneath it — portable code never receives a\n   231\thandle to that stream, and the effect appears as no emission. A v2 append-log\n   232\tbeneath the same synchronous function changes nothing about that. So whether\n   233\tDaoJing's synchronous content handle conforms to Host Boundaries is a real\n   234\tquestion that **this plan does not create and no transport can cure**. J4\n   235\trecords it in `dao.jing.md`'s open items by name, **\"the content write path as\n   236\tan effect stream\"**, with its consequence stated: durability would arrive as\n   237\tdata, and `materialize!`'s return value would change. That redesign is **out of\n   238\tscope for this plan** because it reaches `dao.data.btree.storage` and\n   239\t`dao.space.index`, both of which call `materialize!` synchronously, and the\n   240\tbrief's boundary is `dao.jing*`. Decision 2 removes an unused internal\n   241\ttransport; it does not claim to solve Host Boundaries. It is compatible with\n   242\tthe future design: `datom.world.md:57-59` places raw host operations inside\n   243\tthe host interpreter that consumes the effect stream, which is exactly where\n   244\toption (b) leaves the file IO.\n   245\t\n   246\t**What it costs.** `dao.jing.file` gains a private framed-file section — open\n   247\tor create, truncate an incomplete tail, append a length-prefixed record and\n   248\tsync, replay every record — with a `#?(:cljd … :clj … :cljs …)` branch per host\n   249\t(`RandomAccessFile`, Node's synchronous `fs`, `dart:io`'s `RandomAccessFile`),\n   250\tlifted from `dao.stream.log` by copy, never by require. Roughly one hundred\n   251\tlines. The on-disk format is kept byte-for-byte — 4-byte big-endian length\n   252\tprefix, UTF-8 `pr-str` of `[address payload]` — so every existing content file\n   253\tstays readable, proven by a golden record **and** by the ported torn-tail\n   254\trecovery cases (J1). The copy creates no second owner of the truncation logic\n   255\tbecause J1 retires `dao.stream.log` in the same phase (N1).\n   256\t\n   257\t**What it defers.** A v2 durable-log transport, to whichever plan brings its\n   258\tfirst real consumer: durable observer checkpoints, or a `dao.space` local\n   259\tstream that must survive a restart. The content write path as an effect\n   260\tstream, to a DaoJing architecture item of its own.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '621,900p' docs/design/dao.jing.v2.implementation-plan.md | nl -ba -v621\"","aggregated_output":"   621\tclient is `rpc/client-state` over the same media, wrapped by `client-state`.\n   622\t\n   623\t- put then get round-trips through `step`; duplicate put completes `:present`;\n   624\t  absent get completes `:found? false`; a stored `nil` and a stored\n   625\t  `{:found? true :value \"hello\"}` round-trip exactly.\n   626\t- `request-*` on a non-segment address throws before allocation; a handler\n   627\t  rejecting a hash-mismatched address completes as `:error`.\n   628\t- **`busy` clears only through `step`**: a scripted writer answering `full`\n   629\t  once: `pending-request`; a second request returns `busy` and allocates\n   630\t  nothing; `step` re-attempts and its result carries `requested`; exactly one\n   631\t  id allocated; then a case where the driver only re-calls `request-*` and\n   632\t  asserts `busy` persists.\n   633\t- **unsent at detach**: a request left `pending-request`, then `/detached`\n   634\t  deposited on the response medium; `step` produces exactly one `:lost`\n   635\t  completion for it with `/detached` as the reason, and `:unsent` is empty in\n   636\t  the returned state; after `rebind` the old envelope is never sent.\n   637\t- **`abandon`** before an explicit rebind completes the unsent request with\n   638\t  `:dao.stream.rpc/abandoned`; ids remain monotonic.\n   639\t- gap on the response medium completes every outstanding request as `:lost`\n   640\t  with `:dao.stream/gap`; reader `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor` and\n   641\t  `end` complete as `:lost` with those exact keywords.\n   642\t- `allocator-error` on an exhausted or colliding allocator returns that\n   643\t  outcome, mints no id, and its diagnostic appears in the next `step`'s\n   644\t  `:diagnostics`.\n   645\t- malformed responses complete as `:error` with the malformed-response code\n   646\t  and never as `:found?`; unsolicited responses and decode errors appear in\n   647\t  `:diagnostics`.\n   648\t- **declaration-driven totality**: every keyword `dao.stream.rpc` can emit\n   649\t  as an outcome or reason — enumerated from that namespace's public vars and\n   650\t  its documented reason set — has a branch; completions and diagnostics\n   651\t  publish exactly once and are empty in the state returned after `step`.\n   652\t- `default-handlers` on a handle without `:put-content-fn` throws at\n   653\t  construction.\n   654\t\n   655\t**J3b — over `dao.stream.ws`, same process, all three hosts.** A serving\n   656\tcomposition assembled in the test from `dao.stream.serving` and the host\n   657\t`connect!`/`bind!` seams, exactly as `test/dao/stream/ws_test.cljc` and\n   658\t`serving_test.cljc` do; the served stream is a capacity-1 identity anchor as\n   659\tthe REPL's D3 settled. The client mints its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor on\n   660\tits traffic medium *before* `attach!`, builds the RPC state with\n   661\t`rpc.ws/init-client` from the whole `attach!` result, and wraps it with\n   662\t`client-state`. Prove: put and get round-trip over the wire; two clients see\n   663\teach other's writes; the file backend survives a stop, restart and reattach;\n   664\tkilling the connection completes an *accepted* outstanding request as `:lost`\n   665\twith `/detached`, and **a kill with an unsent request** produces exactly one\n   666\t`:lost` completion and an empty `:unsent` after `rebind` on a fresh `attach!`,\n   667\twith the same deposit cursor resumed; a descriptor naming a path nothing\n   668\tserves completes as `:lost` with `/not-found`.\n   669\t\n   670\t**J3c — the stepped materializer.** `request-materialize` per *Decision 3*,\n   671\tover the J3a primitives, in the same namespace. Tests, socket-free, on all\n   672\tthree hosts:\n   673\t\n   674\t- fresh payload: one put, completes `:inserted` with the derived address.\n   675\t- present payload: put answers `:present`, a second correlated get is issued,\n   676\t  equality holds, completes `:present` with the address; exactly two ids.\n   677\t- present but the server's get returns `:found? false`: completes `:error`\n   678\t  with `/present-but-absent`.\n   679\t- present but unequal (a scripted server store seeded with a different value\n   680\t  at the same address): completes `:error` with `/integrity-failure`.\n   681\t- `:lost` on the verify hop completes the materialization as `:lost` with the\n   682\t  reason; a caller's own `request-get` for the same address during a verify\n   683\t  hop is not confused with it (per-id record).\n   684\t- `busy` during the verify hop behaves as for any request.\n   685\t\n   686\tDeliverable: J3a and J3c green on clj, cljs (Node) and cljd; J3b green on clj\n   687\tand cljs (Node), and on cljd through the cljd ws lane. The cljd test\n   688\tregenerates `test/cljd-out/`; one process owns it at a time.\n   689\t\n   690\t## Phase J4 — Design prose\n   691\t\n   692\t`dao.jing.md`, exactly the sections *Decision 1* names, plus:\n   693\t\n   694\t- *Implemented surface* — the file backend becomes \"a content-addressed store\n   695\t  backed by a private framed append-only file\", replacing \"backed by an\n   696\t  append-only log stream\" (`dao.jing.md:297-299`). This is the design-document\n   697\t  change Decision 2 records; the commit says so. The remote entry describes\n   698\t  `dao.jing.v2.remote` as handlers, stepped primitives, and a stepped\n   699\t  materializer the composition drives, and states that `jing/materialize!`\n   700\t  and `jing/get` are local operations that do not apply to a remote store.\n   701\t- *Reads* — \"may access the target … through a remote transport\" gains \"as a\n   702\t  request whose answer arrives as a completion\".\n   703\t- *Open items* — a new item, **\"The content write path as an effect\n   704\t  stream.\"** `datom.world.md:66-68` classifies a function exposed for portable\n   705\t  code to call as an adapter that keeps the coupling; `:put-content-fn` is\n   706\t  such a function, and `materialize!` returns its address synchronously after\n   707\t  the backend acknowledges. This predates the v2 migration, is not cured by\n   708\t  placing any transport beneath the function, and its resolution is a\n   709\t  redesign in which the content put is an effect on a stream, durability\n   710\t  arrives later as data, and `materialize!`'s return value changes — reaching\n   711\t  every consumer that calls it as a value-returning operation\n   712\t  (`dao.data.btree.storage`, `dao.space.index`). Out of scope for the v2\n   713\t  migration; recorded so it is not mistaken for something the migration\n   714\t  settled.\n   715\t\n   716\t`dao.jing.dht.md` is not edited. The v1 observer's docstrings travel with it to\n   717\t`dao.jing.observer` unedited.\n   718\t\n   719\t## Phase J5 — Deletion and the naming decision, executed\n   720\t\n   721\tGated on `dao.space`'s migration: its tests are the last consumer of v1\n   722\t`dao.jing.observer`, and `dao.jing.coordinate` with its tests the last of v1\n   723\t`dao.jing.remote`. When that gate opens:\n   724\t\n   725\t- delete `src/cljc/dao/jing/observer.cljc` and\n   726\t  `test/dao/jing/observer_test.cljc`;\n   727\t- delete `src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc` and `test/dao/jing/remote_test.cljc`;\n   728\t  `dao.jing.coordinate`'s `:dao.jing/remote` branch is deleted or re-pointed\n   729\t  under `dao.space`'s plan, not here;\n   730\t- rename `dao.jing.v2.observer` → `dao.jing.observer` and `dao.jing.v2.remote`\n   731\t  → `dao.jing.remote`, with their tests, executing Decision 4. Whatever\n   732\t  `dao.stream` decides about its own name is not a prerequisite.\n   733\t\n   734\tAn undecided coexistence is a defect of the migration, not a steady state;\n   735\tthis plan has decided, and J5 executes.\n   736\t\n   737\t## Host matrix\n   738\t\n   739\t| phase | clj | cljs (Node) | cljd | notes                                                                                     |\n   740\t| ----- | --- | ----------- | ---- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   741\t| J1    | ✓   | ✓           | ✓    | three host branches in `dao.jing.file`, `:cljd` first; torn-tail fixtures hand-written per host as in `log_test.cljc` |\n   742\t| J2    | ✓   | ✓           | ✓    | pure `.cljc`, no host branch                                                              |\n   743\t| J3a   | ✓   | ✓           | ✓    | pure `.cljc`, no host branch                                                              |\n   744\t| J3b   | ✓   | ✓           | ✓    | composition uses the per-host `connect!` seam; cljd through the cljd ws lane              |\n   745\t| J3c   | ✓   | ✓           | ✓    | pure `.cljc`, no host branch                                                              |\n   746\t| J4    | —   | —           | —    | prose                                                                                     |\n   747\t| J5    | ✓   | ✓           | ✓    | deletions and renames; confirm every `dao.jing*` test namespace still appears per host    |\n   748\t\n   749\tPer the standing rule, confirm `Testing dao.jing.v2.observer-test` and\n   750\t`Testing dao.jing.v2.remote-test` appear in the Node output rather than\n   751\tassuming discovery.\n   752\t\n   753\t## Invariants, checked\n   754\t\n   755\t- **No hidden global state.** `dao.jing.v2.observer` holds no atom.\n   756\t  `dao.jing.file`'s state atom and lock are per-handle and explicit, as\n   757\t  `dao.jing.mem`'s are. No `defopen`, no dispatch table, no `defonce`; J1\n   758\t  removes one `defopen` from the ambient v1 registry.\n   759\t- **No implicit control flow.** `observe-step!` and `step` return; the\n   760\t  composition calls again. Nothing self-schedules.\n   761\t- **No callbacks.** None in any new public surface. The ws adapter map is the\n   762\t  transport's, confined to J3b's composition.\n   763\t- **No shared mutable state.** A content handle is owned by whoever created\n   764\t  it; the RPC state is a value threaded by one driver.\n   765\t- **Interpretation and execution stay separate.** The observer interprets the\n   766\t  pool; the backend executes the write; the remote client interprets\n   767\t  completions; the serving composition executes the socket.\n   768\t- **No assumed graphs.** Nothing here relates content addresses to each other.\n   769\t\n   770\t## Boundary of this plan\n   771\t\n   772\t**Untouched:** `dao.jing.mem`; v1 `dao.jing.remote` until J5;\n   773\t`dao.jing.coordinate`; `dao.jing.dht`, `dao.jing.dht.kad`, `dao.jing.dht.node`;\n   774\tevery `dao.space*` and `dao.data.btree*` `src/` namespace; `dao.stream` v1 in\n   775\tevery form except `dao.stream.log`.\n   776\t\n   777\t**Touched outside `dao.jing*`, both scope-contingent, both decided at\n   778\tauthorization:**\n   779\t\n   780\t- five `test/dao/space/` files, one require line and alias each (J1.1);\n   781\t  alternative: the documentation route, with transitive v1 stated as an\n   782\t  end-condition bullet until J5.\n   783\t- `src/cljc/dao/stream/log.cljc` and `test/dao/stream/log_test.cljc`, deleted\n   784\t  (J1.3); alternative: stream-plan-owned \"legacy append-log retirement\",\n   785\t  coordinated atomically with J1.\n   786\t\n   787\t**Architectural decisions**, made here: the durable log is not a stream and\n   788\tthe write-path redesign is a separate item (Decision 2); remote content is a\n   789\tdriven state machine with a stepped materializer, not a handle (Decision 3);\n   790\tthe observer's cursor and non-progress discipline (Decision 1); the\n   791\tthree-namespace end state and the observer move-out (Decision 4).\n   792\t\n   793\t**Implementation gaps**, left open knowingly: the v2 remote client has no\n   794\tcoordinate and cannot be reached from `dao.jing.coordinate/open!`; the\n   795\tserving side exists only as test compositions; the promise-shaped\n   796\t`hydrate-async`/`store-tree-async` of `dao.data.btree.md` §5.4 is a driver\n   797\tover this client that this plan does not build.\n   798\t\n   799\t**Intentionally deferred**, each to a named home:\n   800\t\n   801\t- **The content write path as an effect stream** — a DaoJing architecture\n   802\t  item recorded in J4; it reaches `dao.data.btree.storage` and\n   803\t  `dao.space.index` and is not a migration.\n   804\t- `dao.jing.dht` and `dao.jing.dht.node` — UDP transport, deferred by the\n   805\t  stream plan; `dht.cljc` itself requires no `dao.stream`, and `node.cljc`'s\n   806\t  `dao.stream.transit` require goes with the UDP transport's own migration.\n   807\t- `dao.space`'s own migration: `publish-index!`'s v1 `ds/append!` into the\n   808\t  intake pool, `snapshot-datoms`' `{:position 0}` walk, the published-index\n   809\t  `defopen`, `DaoStreamLog`'s writer face, and switching its tests from\n   810\t  `dao.jing.observer` (v1) to the v2 observer.\n   811\t- The `:dao.jing/remote` coordinate under v2 and the async hydration driver\n   812\t  (`dao.data.btree.md` §5.4) — `dao.space`'s query-side plan; read hydration\n   813\t  needs J3a, `store-tree-async` needs J3c.\n   814\t- A content-serving endpoint as production infrastructure — the composition\n   815\t  that starts, serves and stops a content server belongs to whatever product\n   816\t  surface needs it, as the REPL's R4 did for the REPL.\n   817\t- Canonical encoding; durable observer checkpoints and a long-running runner;\n   818\t  explicit materialization acknowledgement; garbage collection — the open\n   819\t  items of `dao.jing.md`, unchanged. Checkpoints gain only the shape\n   820\t  constraint that the persisted value is the transport's own cursor, whose\n   821\t  serializability the contract leaves TBD.\n   822\t- A v2 file or append-log transport — until a consumer needs cursors over a\n   823\t  durable file.\n   824\t\n   825\t## End condition\n   826\t\n   827\tComplete when all of the following hold on clj, cljs (Node) and cljd:\n   828\t\n   829\t- `dao.jing` requires no `dao.stream*` namespace. `dao.jing.file` requires no\n   830\t  `dao.stream*` namespace, reads every content file v1 wrote including a\n   831\t  torn one, and its suite runs with no stream fixture. `dao.stream.log` is\n   832\t  gone, or its retirement is a named, atomically coordinated stream-plan\n   833\t  dependency landed with J1.\n   834\t- `dao.jing.v2.observer` observes v2 reader handles with stream-minted\n   835\t  cursors, is total over `dao.stream/outcomes-next` by a\n   836\t  declaration-driven test, retains only successors and adopted cursors, and\n   837\t  requires only `dao.jing` and `dao.stream`.\n   838\t- `dao.jing.v2.remote` round-trips put, get and **materialize** through\n   839\t  `dao.stream.apply` and `dao.stream.rpc` over ring buffers on all three\n   840\t  hosts and over `dao.stream.ws` on each; clears `busy` only through\n   841\t  `step`; completes every lost, abandoned and unsent-at-terminal request as\n   842\t  data exactly once; and publishes diagnostics exactly once.\n   843\t- `dao.jing.md` describes the v2 observer, the stream-free file backend, the\n   844\t  driven remote client with its materializer, and records the write-path\n   845\t  question as an open item.\n   846\t- No namespace under `dao.jing.v2*` requires `dao.stream` or\n   847\t  `dao.stream.rpc.*`, **directly or transitively** — or, under the\n   848\t  documentation route, `dao.jing.v2*` is transitively v1 until J5, stated as\n   849\t  this bullet.\n   850\t- Once `dao.space` has migrated: v1 `dao.jing.observer` and v1\n   851\t  `dao.jing.remote` are deleted, and `dao.jing.v2.observer` and\n   852\t  `dao.jing.v2.remote` are renamed to `dao.jing.observer` and\n   853\t  `dao.jing.remote`.\n   854\t\n   855\t---\n   856\t\n   857\t## What changed from the round-1 draft, and why\n   858\t\n   859\t- **A1 / C5 — `step` ordering and abandonment.** Round 1 specified a `busy`\n   860\t  guard and a `step` that only polled; since `rpc/request!` is the sole path\n   861\t  that re-attempts an unsent envelope and `lose-outstanding` covers\n   862\t  `:outstanding` only, `busy` could never clear and an unsent request was\n   863\t  carried silently across `rebind`. `step` now re-attempts, polls, abandons on\n   864\t  terminal, then publishes; `abandon` is exposed; both cases are tested.\n   865\t  Cause: reasoning from the sibling plans' prose instead of `rpc.cljc`.\n   866\t- **A2 — `:diagnostics` in the step result**, published exactly once; round 1\n   867\t  claimed to take them and had nowhere to put them.\n   868\t- **A3 / N1 — torn-tail coverage and the orphaned transport.** Round 1's\n   869\t  golden record proved clean replay only while copying the truncation loops\n   870\t  out of `dao.stream.log`; the only recovery test would have died with v1.\n   871\t  J1 now ports `torn-tail-test` with the fixture adaptation (a valid Jing\n   872\t  record first, since `[11 22]` is not decodable) plus sub-four-byte and\n   873\t  negative-length cases, and retires `dao.stream.log` in the same phase so\n   874\t  the framing has one owner. Scope-contingent, with the atomic stream-plan\n   875\t  alternative named and \"leave it standing\" excluded.\n   876\t- **C1 — Decision 2 stands, with two concessions.** It now states that it\n   877\t  amends `dao.jing.md`'s own description of the file backend, routed as a\n   878\t  design change; records the pre-existing Host-Boundaries question about the\n   879\t  synchronous content handle as an open item that no transport cures; and\n   880\t  explains via the no-wait rule why option (a) is the write-path redesign in\n   881\t  disguise rather than a like-for-like transport.\n   882\t- **C2 / C4 — the observer moves out; the end state is decided.** The fact\n   883\t  that the v1 observer has no `src/` caller made \"move the observer out\"\n   884\t  cheaper than \"extract the core\" by nine production namespaces. `dao.jing`\n   885\t  is stream-free from J1; the transitive gate is literal; the end state is\n   886\t  three namespaces, decided now, independent of `dao.stream`'s naming.\n   887\t  Seven test repoints, not five; five are scope-contingent with the\n   888\t  documentation route named.\n   889\t- **C3 — the materializer.** Round 1's `request-put` was the backend primitive\n   890\t  and was called a DaoJing backend; it omitted derivation and read-back\n   891\t  verification. J3a keeps the primitives, J3c adds `request-materialize`, the\n   892\t  end condition includes it, and the §5.4 claim is narrowed to \"a driver over\n   893\t  this client, not built here\".\n   894\t- **C6 — completion totality by construction**: reason pass-through,\n   895\t  `allocator-error` in the request outcome set, declaration-driven test.\n   896\t- **Line reference fix:** `jing_test.cljc` observer section is lines 322–560.\n   897\t- **Not changed:** Decision 1 in substance, the in-place rewrite of\n   898\t  `dao.jing.file`, the deferral of the `:dao.jing/remote` coordinate to\n   899\t  `dao.space`'s plan, and the phase ordering J1 → J2 → J3 → J4 → J5. Both\n   900\t  reviews and both consensus seats found these sound.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
